  <CaseSection id="process" title="How it was built">
    <CaseProse>
      <p>
        The wake word is detected on the device, so no audio leaves it just to hear
        “log a tot.” Providers asked about that first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Speech is matched against known phrases and the class roster, with no AI in
        the loop. A live model would be slower and could log a nap that never
        happened; the rules either match or send the entry to review.
      </p>
      <p>
        AI runs earlier: when a child is added, it lists likely mishearings of the
        name, and review-queue corrections extend that list. Spanish is translated
        before matching, so one phrase set covers both languages.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <CaseCards label="Services" items={integrations.map((s) => ({ icon: s.icon, title: s.name, body: s.role }))} />

    <CaseProse class="mt-12">
      <p>
        Attendance isn’t stored as a flag. It’s calculated from check-in history, so
        it can’t drift from what happened, because a wrong headcount is a licensing
        problem.
      </p>
    </CaseProse>

    <div class="mt-12">
      <p class="mono-label mb-6">System-level decisions</p>
      <ul class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
